e Republic of China (1912-1949) had been created as the provisional government. Sun Yat-sen was announced as President, but for less than a year before Yuan Shikai, commander of the New Army and Prime Minister under the Qing Government, took his place. In 1915, Yuan declared himself emperor, and his short-lived attempt at a monarchy was upset by various rebellions, for which he abdicated his position in 1916. In Japan, the Meiji government experienced a quick transition towards an industrialized economy, and this economic stability, coupled with Japans increasing (and healthier, longer-living population), saw Japan develop into a strong democratic institution whose international power was growing by the Taisho period. The relationships between China and Japan during this period grew byzantine. In 1914, Chinas Parliament was formally dissolved, and Yuan produced a ``constitutional compact{''} that essentially replaced Chinas provisional constitution, which gave Yuan, as president, virtually unlimited powers over Chinas military, finances, foreign policy, and the rights of the Chinese citizens. Yuan would justify these reforms by stating that representative democracy had been proven inefficient by political infighting. Furthermore, Japan issued China the Twenty-One Demands on January 8, 1915, which would extend Japanese control over Manchuria, and it would give the Japanese control over the Chinese economy. The result of these demands deleteriously influenced Japans relationship not just with China (the Chinese boycotted Japanese exports, which fell by 40\%), but also with the U.S. and Britain, both of whom saw the move as a domineering approach to diplomacy. The two western countries would also reevaluate their connection to the Japanese after the demands, which retroactively affected Japans ties with Britain, especially in light that the former had signed an important military alliance with the latter earlier in the century..
